e":"This turns the IMF's debt ratios into US dollars: each economy's debt as a share of its GDP, times its GDP, at the end-of-year exchange rate, so the amounts can be added up. It counts debt once, by the borrower; it is never added to money, because a bank loan and the deposit it creates are the same dollar. The IMF's figures reach 121 economies in 2024 and 103 in 1990, so part of the rise over the decades is economies joining the data. Years left out because the dollar amount fails the check against the IMF's own dollar GDP (usually a currency unit that differs between two IMF tables, or a redenomination): Argentina 1950-1962; Azerbaijan 1992; Bulgaria 1991-2024; Cameroon 1994; Democratic Republic of the Congo 2000-2001; Dominican Republic 1950-1979, 1983; Ecuador 1950-1999; Egypt 1990-1991; Estonia 1992; Ethiopia 1992; Georgia 1995; Ghana 1955-1979; Guatemala 1986; Guyana 1990; Honduras 1990-1999; Iran 1985-1987, 1991-1992, 2012, 2018-2023; Kuwait 1991; Lebanon 2020-2023; Liberia 1974-1989, 1991-2022; Moldova 1991-1994; Mali 1960-1981, 1989; Myanmar 1960-2012; Nigeria 1951-2023; Nicaragua 1987, 1990; Russia 1995; Sudan 1960-1979, 1981-1982, 1990-1991, 2012, 2014-2020; Sierra Leone 1960-2024; El Salvador 1950-2000; Serbia 1999-2000; South Sudan 2017; Suriname 1990-1993, 2020; Tanzania 1986-1987; Uganda 1960-1990; Uruguay 1964-1979; Venezuela 1950-2015; Yemen 1990-2013; Zambia 1965-1979; Zimbabwe 1979, 1981-2005, 2019-2023. It counts loans and debt securities of households and non-financial companies. Euro members' GDP is stated in euros for every year, so before a country joined the euro its national rate is divided by the fixed conversion rate. Source: International Monetary Fund."}